t have  much i remember always being very proud  of the wall-to-wall shelves of books my  family had  the books allowed me to escape my narrow  world of  long food lines for the magnificent  worlds experienced by the heroes in my  books  our books are boxes arrived three months  later mostly intact except for two  things that didn't make a journey  a folder full of my art and a box of my  books  as for my art i gradually accepted my  parents explanation that my art was  confiscated by the authorities  because it represented valuable cultural  artifacts forbidden to leave russia  one of these these days i'm sure i'll  find them in the hermitage  i was devastated by the loss of my books  we were among the early wave of the mass  migration of soviet jews to israel  and when we arrived there were no  russian language books to be found  anywhere and so i was left bookless  the irony of the russian immigrants to  israel that in russia we were never  russians we were jews  it said so in our identity cards in  israel however we were never israelis  we were russian the reality that they  will never truly belong in this new  country  became evident to me within the first  few days in my new school when i arrived  to school wearing a traditional russian  first day of school dress with balls in  my hair  just to find everyone else dressed  comfortably in sweatpants  i was desperate to belong in mastering  the language was my entry ticket  at first i had a slow start for my first  few months we were in israel my mom paid  me five cents per page to motivate me to  read children's picture books  i went from reading around the world in  80 days in russian  to laboriously decoding a red pyjama in  hebrew  one word at a time it was painful and  demoralizing and i felt like a bird  without wings  i was ready to pack up my belongings and  travel back to russia in pursuit of my  lost art  but the week before summer vacation my  class took a trip to local library to  pick out our summer 