n his eyes trying his  hardest  to read through a passage word by word  by word just like i did so many years  ago  but for him this wasn't a fleeting  hiccup but the beginning of a lifelong  struggle  this experience sparked my passion to  unravel the neural mechanisms of  individual differences in reading  development  in the research here at mcgovern  institute we've been discovering the  children at risk for developmental  dyslexia show brain alterations  often associated with this disorder as  early as first few months of  kindergarten  despite substantial hereditary and  behavioral risk however some children  proceed to develop typical reading  skills and the research shows that these  children often recruit alternative  neural pathways and use different  cognitive strategies for reading  so i'm interested in figuring out these  mechanisms of risk and resilience in  reading development  because i believe that reading is not  only a privilege and an anchor as it was  for me  but a basic human right that all  children should enjoy  thank you  yay to ola thank you so much for being  our first storyteller i  love that story and i have such a clear  image of the determined little girl  who loves bubble gum and wants to find a  way to read  thank you for bringing her into the room  with you tonight  our next storyteller is jill crittenden  and when i asked jill what she would  stockpile if she could go back in time  to march she answered  stock in zoom but she added she is not  ready to tell us what she would do with  the prophets  please welcome jill thank you  for the rest is usually considered a  good thing it takes perseverance to  learn skills  like riding a bike every time you fall  off you have to get back on  again and again in fact if you've ever  had dinner with a toddler  you know that even a simple behavior  like feeding yourself takes perseverance  and repetition  after a while the whole sequence of  movements required for whatever task  you're doing seems effortless  and we just go on autopilot but  sometimes that autopilot is our own  worst enemy and  unconsciously initiates harmful habits  like nail biting or smoking  in many brain disorders such as autism  and schizophrenia repetitive behaviors  can be severe and very difficult to  disrupt  people become so stuck doing the same  thing over and over again  that they seem completely unaware of the  world around them  a few years ago i was studying the  biological basis of these extreme  repetitive behaviors in ann graybiel's  lab  and we made a remarkable discovery about  why they might sometimes be so hard to  disrupt  that's interesting in and of itself but  the resilience part of my story  is about what happened when we tried to  publish our discovery  we were doing experiments to test which  neurons in the brain disrupt a habit  for example if someone says stop biting  your nails which neurons hear it  and make you stop we were using  genetically engineered mice to study  this so that we could actually control  the activity of specific brain cells to  figure out which  ones disrupt the autopilot to induce the  repetitive behaviors we used amphetamine  you might or might not know that  amphetamine causes really  extreme repetitive behaviors that are  really hard to disrupt like skin picking  or pacing  our big discovery was that these extreme  repetitive behaviors  were not interruptible like they should  be the autopilot was deaf  and could not be turned off when you  were exposed to amphetamine  our discovery suggested that in other  cases of pathological repetitive  behaviors  when people are very engaged and seem to  not even  hear the people who are trying to  interrupt them in fact  the habit part of their brain might not  even be getting the signal that  somebody's trying to talk to them  when we were writing up this exciting  discovery for publication  kaboom a canadian group unexpectedly  published a paper  about the genetically engineered mice we  had used  and they said that these mice when they  were trying to solve a task  in a maze kept turning the wrong way  over and over again  bug what did that mean about the  discovery we'd made about repetitive  behaviors using these very same mice  our team had an emergency meeting and we  figured out how to edit our manuscript  and incorporate the canadian group's  findings into our paper  and then we sent it to a talk journal  and we waited  let me tell you a bit about the  manuscript review process it has  multiple steps  the first one is to interest the journal  editors enough to send your paper out  for review by other scientists  we succeeded with that step the second  hurdle is peer review  when experts in your field critique your  manuscript line by line  there are often only two reviewers of  your manuscript but a third reviewer  might be added as a tiebreaker  if the first two don't see eye to eye so  reviewer number three  is the term that's often used to the  most harsh critic of your work  finally our reviews were back and we had  three of them  i carefully read through the requested  changes to our manuscript line by line  but when i got to reviewer number three  my heart sank  number three said that what the canadian  group had said about the genetically  engineered mice that we'd used was a  deal breaker  and that we had to repeat all of our  experiments with a completely different  method this would take many many months  moreover a key member of our team was  just about to move  away to california i felt like reviewer  number three had broken both my kneecaps  but i found out i could still stand back  up  and go in the lab well we set up the new  experimental methods and looked for a  replacement team member  we looked more closely at the behavioral  mice we decided to use  and we found that there repetitive  behaviors in fact went on much longer  than in normal mice  and we found that the genetic mutation  in them  actually might be related to this