ine to  capture these vocalizations in the wild  machine learning algorithms to classify  them and we're developing a tool that  shares the meaning behind these sounds  in real time  to enhance the communication between  non-verbal individuals  and the world we're trying to change the  question from  why don't they speak to how do  they communicate here's the thing  there is a lot that felix can't do  but there's also so much that he can  in life we often don't get to choose the  path we're on  but we do get to choose what we're going  to do on the journey  thank you so much christy for that  beautiful story and thank you  so much for introducing us all to felix  and for being the sort of person who  faces a problem  head-on and with so much love please  welcome our next storyteller  merti ramadan when i asked him what he  would stockpile if he could go back in  time too much  he said something that i think we all  would love a little bit more of these  days  clorox wipes please welcome merci  thank you my story begins with my mom's  sister and i being evacuated by u.s  marines from war-torn lebanon to cyprus  i remember finding a cotton candy  machine on the battleship we were on  and just for a fleeting moment i could  enjoy being a kid not a kid terrified  of what he's just experienced not a kid  terrified of what lies ahead  after five long days we finally arrived  in the seattle airport  and a strong smell of coffee welcomed us  to our new home  and our new way of life now this might  seem like a triumphant moment  that now we're saved but it's not always  that simple  as we made our way home i remember a mix  of emotion  we were joyful and relieved but at the  same time nervous  are we going to be okay here  with sudden change come sudden maturity  and even at a  young age of 11 years old i realized i  had opportunities here in the united  states i didn't have  back in lebanon i was absolutely  fascinated by  the brain and mathematics and i dreamt  that one day  i want to go to college and i want to  become a neuroscientist just like my  hero oliver sacks  as we settled into life in seattle  everything seemed  bright but then one day our foundation  came crashing down my dad had his second  heart attack  but this time it was different this time  he lost his job medical bills piled up  we actually had to relocate from our  home to government housing  to afford rent i felt as if the ground  beneath us  began to crumble and i started asking  myself  am i going to be okay will i still be  able to attend college one day  i'm still going to become a  neuroscientist right  as the years passed by my dream of  attending college became more like a  figment of  my imagination started noticing  a growing gap between myself and my  friends  while they during their summers went to  summer camps for robotics coding science  and sports  i stayed home while they received new  computers and video  game consoles for their birthdays i did  not  while they started touring college  campuses with their families and getting  private tutors to help them with the  sats and personal statements my family  could barely afford a car to get around  with  at this time i was nearing the end of my  high school years  so i had to face my reality and make a  decision  is college still a possibility for me or  should i  pursue something more practical and get  a job and support my family financially  um and i had discussions with my family  about college and it was  full of uncertainty questions like even  if i get into college who's going to pay  for it  what are these scholarships and are they  a scam  why in neuroscience maybe i could pick  something easier  what if this whole college thing this  whole  university this whole academia science  thing it's just not made for families  like us a pivotal moment came  when one of our family friends laura who  had known me since i was  two years old and knew how bad i wanted  to go to college  basically forced me to apply uh for a  high school summer research internship  at the university of washington i was so  sure  that i wouldn't get into the internship  why pick me out of all the kids  but to my surprise i got chosen  the the the feeling that somebody out  there actually saw potential in me  even though at that time i didn't see  potential in myself  is what inspired me to actually go to  the internship  now on the the end of the first day of  my internship  i thought i had made a huge huge huge  mistake  the kids around me they were so smart so  confident  they already knew how to code it's it's  as if they've trained for this  moment their whole life i i just didn't  belong there  i should go back on the second day of  the internship  i learned i was placed in a lab that  dealt with  a left ventricular assist device which  is a system that you implant for heart  failure patients  and in that moment i really thought  about my dad and his heart problems  and that intrigued me what is this  device and how could i ever contribute  to this device into these people  and that's what pushed me to go in the  third day  on the third day i was given my first  task which is to code up  a small piece of code to simulate the  heat properties of one of  the components that have to go inside  the patient and  we have to make sure that it doesn't  burn the tissue of the patient  i'm terrified i've never coded before  i've never even opened a program before  why did they pick me why not just  another student who should do it  but at that moment i had to stop and  think  what if i don't take this opportunity  what would my life look like  what if i'm forever constrained by the  the financial circumstances that were  handed down to me  but in that moment i decided to take  control  and it took me two weeks to create this  little piece of code  but in those two weeks i created that  piece of code and little did i know  that this little piece of code would  create the  coil design for the component that  selected out of many in the lab  would end up being used for the first  clinical trials the system underwent  and not only that but it turned out  later on that my coil design was as  close to optimal  as it could be this is the first time  that i felt the ground  solidify and that the first step i took  in this path  wasn't muddy that it didn't sink me and  i didn't make a fool of  myself that actually this is a path that  i can take  and that i want to take i could go back  to my family and say  that i have a contribution and impact to  make in the world and that i can do it  and that college might just be the thing  for me  inspired i uh got into  applied and gotten to the university of  washington  took part of ever more computational uh  pieces of work in research anything from  seizure predictions to models of visual  cortex  and i'm proud to say that here at mit  i've actually  had the privilege of teaching  programming and how to code to incoming  graduate students in the department  and now as part of the jazieri lab i  studied the computational basis of  counter-factual thinking  the same sort of cognitive processes  that went on in that pivotal  moment when i thought what if i take  this opportunity  but what if i don't and today i share my  story  for those of you out there who feel like  me and i want to tell you  that you don't have to be constrained by  the financial circumstances you were  born in  that you can follow the passions of your  heart and the most important thing you  have is not money or connections  but the belief people have in you and  the belief that you have in yourself  and i'm here to say to you that i  believe in you  thank you  thank you so much maddie for such a  wonderful  journey that from being on a boat with  the marines  to the jazz lab at mit there's an  incredible story thank you so much  our final storyteller for tonight is  heather kozikowski  and when i asked her what she would  stockpile that she could go back in time  to march  she said and i feel like we're all with  her too hugs  because she's so misses seeing people  please welcome  heather  have you ever experienced hate  not the haters gonna hate kind of hate  but someone's deep  loathing directed toward you when  someone hits you  that much their face becomes so  distorted with rage  they are unrecognizable violence is  inevitable  and if you happen to be brave enough or  stupid enough  to look into that person's eyes well  there are no words to describe the  intensity of hate-filled eyes  in that moment the object of hatred  becomes just that  an object and ceases to exist  at least that's how it felt for me my  mom had the ability to knead me  with one breath and in the next be  filled with such rage and utter hate  that bruises were inevitable  and my life was in peril how do you  survive  the hatred of your own mother  resilience great perseverance  those words have marked my entire life  and they are not words i am proud of  behind those words are a lot of pain  a lot of overcoming a lot of surviving  sexual physical and mental abuse  homeless shelters welfare foster care  the marine corps systems and structures  that broke me  failure after failure after failure  and somehow for some unknown reason  i have still fought still persevered  still survived  my whole life i only ever dreamed of two  things going to college and having a  family  at the age of 26 i started taking  classes at the local community college  a few short months later i was pregnant  i dropped out a baby  messed up little me was going to have a  baby  how could someone like me that has never  been loved  ever give love when she was born  my sweet little baby girl i was filled  with such awe  and wonder in the maternity ward i would  carefully swaddle her place her in her  bassinet and just stare  her cute little cheeks her sweet little  nose her perfect little eyes  wrapped in her knitted purple blanket  and matching hat that nurses came went  came and went every few hours one  particularly puffy a nurse bounded and  oh my gosh she is so sweet i bet you are  just hugging her and kissing her  all the time then she took her vitals  and left  kissing her i can do that  she was two days old and it had never  once occurred to me to kiss  my own daughter right then and there i  tried it  it felt awkward at first this learning  to love thing  but also right i started to learn all  the mom things baby baths and bouncing  and burping and diapers and swaddles  one day when she was just about six  weeks old she cried  and cried and cried and there was  nothing  literally nothing i could do as she  cried i made all sorts of promises to  her  i told her it was okay mommy was there  she was safe i told her that i love her  and in that moment i knew it was true  a few months later the afternoon  sunlight was streaming through the  window  with smashed avocado and sweet potato  stains all over the counter i looked  down into this sweet baby girl's eyes  into my daughter's eyes and i realized  i want the world for her i want her to  know that she can do anything she wants  looking at her i realize that if i want  her to truly believe she can accomplish  her dreams  i would have to accomplish mine and so i  set out on a journey of self-discovery  of academic inquiry i came back to the  question that has haunted me since  childhood  what drives people to hate  a few years later i found the paper that  transformed my thinking  i had already learned that hate happens  when we failed to see the humanity in  others  but this paper explained the neural  mechanisms that enable us to actually  see someone else's pain then it occurred  to me  if i want to understand what happens  when thing goes things go wrong  when people learn to hate i have to  start at the beginning  before we learn to hate i have to start  with babies  have you ever looked into the eyes of a  baby oh my gosh  if you have it you need to try it there  is nothing more heavenly in this world  babies give and receive love at any  moment  with any human not yet closed off to the  realities of this harsh world human  babies have a way of  innocently connected with anyone that's  willing and saying  i am here with you and i see you  as you are here with me it  is powerful so i set out to better  understand babies  the place where we all begin before we  learn to hate but are capable of immense  love  how do babies see the world my person to  understand how infant brains support  their minds  is a new dream when i wouldn't have even  their dream that fateful day in my  kitchen so many years ago  it has been hard so hard  i have cried a lot i have lost  a lot but i have also shown my daughter  that not only can she achieve achieve  her dreams  she can have dreams beyond her wildest  imagination  you all i am a phd student at mit do you  know how unheard of it is for a foster  kid to get a bachelor's degree let alone  a phd  and yet here i am a foster kid phd  candidate at mit  doing the kind of research that only a  handful of people in the world  have even attempted over the last few  years i have held  comforted swaddled and looked into the  eyes  and brains of over a hundred babies  with every ounce of my being i want to  know what is happening  in their mind right now and with each  baby that i meet  i stare into their eyes just as deeply  as they look into mine and make sure  they know  just like my daughter did that i am  there  with them seeing them as they  see me  heather thank you so much for such a  beautiful story  it reminds me to spend a little bit more  time  looking into my baby's eyes and thank  you so much for being so generous  in sharing it with us tonight now before  our judges get started  on figuring out a winner i want to give  you a  quick recap of everything that we've  heard tonight  in moving from russia to israel ola lost  her ability to read  a moment that shaped her work as a  researcher  jill overcame rejection from that  dreaded reviewer number three  christy told us of a complete career  change to develop technology to help her  son  melty overcame incredible odds and  self-doubt to pursue  a dream of coding after a promise to her  father  shannon secured his career led her to  the boyden lab and who what knows where  next deepon's late night eureka moment  in the lab cemented his ability as an  independent researcher  and finally heather told us how she  found a new lease on life  after the birth of her daughter  and now our judges maddie and tina will  be  selecting today's winning story they've  already ducked into a magical  behind the scenes green room for the  next two minutes  and while they're gone you'll be able to  see some photos  submitted by our storytellers that  illustrate each of our storytellers  lives and stories and now while we can't  see you tonight  we have a chat function open and all of  our storytellers can see it  so while our judges are deliberating  please let us know if you're having a  good time  and which stories you loved and we'll  see you back here in two minutes  three years ago  oh yeah  [Music]  [Music]  oh  [Music]  [Music]  okay  [Music]  [Music]  down  [Music]  your dreams  [Music]  okay  [Music]  [Music]  so  so  welcome back to our judges maddie and  tina  um that took slightly longer because  they had a really unenviable task of  having to decide  who would be the winner so thank you so  much and i will hand it over to both of  them for their decision  okay so that was ridiculously difficult  um i want to say first of all thank you  to all of our storytellers it is  not easy to tell a story it is not easy  to tell a personal story and it is not  easy to tell a story over zoom i will  say that  for sure so you know we were judging on  a lot of different  themes we were dodging on themes  originality um  and it was a really tough decision  because i feel like  so many of our storytellers today nailed  